    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d103">

                <pb facs="0215" n="119" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg119"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Pekin, China</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-05-03"><hi rendition="#italic">May 3,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 367.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Department’s instructions,
                    dated March 17th [sent 15th] last, I have the honor to report that the U. S. S.
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Wheeling</hi> arrived on the bar off Taku April 7,
                    and that on the 30th I received <pb facs="0216" n="120" xml:id="pg120"/> the
                    following telegram from her commanding officer, William T. Burwell: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Wheeling</hi> leaves (for) Shanghai immediately.
                            Cable reads: Inform minister, Department so directs, and if serious need
                            arises for relief vessel, communicate with Kempff, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Newark</hi>, Yokohama.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Conger.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
